tem"><a href="/encyclopedia/johann-lachmann"><span class="enc-name">Johann Lachmann</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Johann Lachmann was a Lutheran reformer who, as preacher in the imperial city of Heilbronn, led its turn to the Reformation, completed in 1528.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/johann-ludwig-burckhardt"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/johann-ludwig-burckhardt.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Johann Ludwig Burckhardt</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Johann Ludwig Burckhardt was a Swiss traveller and orientalist who explored the Near East in the guise of a Muslim scholar, Sheikh Ibrahim.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/johann-ludwig-krapf"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/johann-ludwig-krapf.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Johann Ludwig Krapf</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Johann Ludwig Krapf was a German missionary of the Church Missionary Society who founded the first Protestant mission in East Africa at Rabai near Mombasa in 1846.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/johann-lukas-von-hildebrandt"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/johann-lukas-von-hildebrandt.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Johann Lukas von Hildebrandt</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Johann Lukas von Hildebrandt was an Austrian Baroque architect, born in Genoa, who became court architect in Vienna.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/johann-martin-honigberger"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/johann-martin-honigberger.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Johann Martin Honigberger</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Johann Martin Honigberger was a physician from Transylvania who served at the court of Ranjit Singh in Lahore.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/johann-matthias-von-der-schulenburg"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/johann-matthias-von-der-schulenburg.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Johann Matthias von der Schulenburg</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Johann Matthias von der Schulenburg was a German soldier who served Saxony and other powers before becoming field marshal of the Republic of Venice in 1715.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/johann-michael-fischer"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/johann-michael-fischer.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Johann Michael Fischer</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Johann Michael Fischer was a Bavarian architect, one of the leading builders of the south German Baroque and Rococo.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/johann-of-manderscheid-blankenheim"><span class="enc-name">Johann of Manderscheid-Blankenheim</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Johann of Manderscheid-Blankenheim (1538–1592) was Bishop of Strasbourg from 1569.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/johann-reinhard-iii-of-hanau"><span class="enc-name">Johann Reinhard III of Hanau</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Johann Reinhard III was the last count of Hanau, ruling Hanau-Lichtenberg from 1680 and also Hanau-Münzenberg from 1712.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/johann-reinhold-patkul"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/johann-reinhold-patkul.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Johann Reinhold Patkul</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Johann Reinhold Patkul was a Livonian nobleman who led the opposition of the Baltic German nobility to Charles XI's reduction of their estates.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/johann-rudolf-wettstein"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/johann-rudolf-wettstein.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Johann Rudolf Wettstein</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Johann Rudolf Wettstein was mayor of Basel from 1645 and the leading Swiss diplomat of his time.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/johann-sebastian-bach"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/johann-sebastian-bach.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Johann Sebastian Bach</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Johann Sebastian Bach was a German composer and organist, born at Eisenach.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/johann-smidt"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/johann-smidt.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Johann Smidt</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Johann Smidt was a Bremen theologian and statesman who served many terms as the city's mayor between 1821 and 1857.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/johann-tserclaes-count-of-tilly"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/johann-tserclaes-count-of-tilly.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Johann Tserclaes, Count of Tilly</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Johann Tserclaes, Count of Tilly, was a Walloon general who commanded the army of the Catholic League in the Thirty Years' War.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/johann-von-munchhausen"><span class="enc-name">Johann von Münchhausen</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Johann von Münchhausen was a Livonian churchman of a Lower Saxon noble family who held the bishoprics of Ösel-Wiek and Courland.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/johann-weikhard-von-valvasor"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/johann-weikhard-von-valvasor.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Johann Weikhard von Valvasor</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Johann Weikhard von Valvasor was a Carniolan nobleman and polymath, born in Ljubljana.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/johann-wilhelm-ludwig-gleim"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/johann-wilhelm-ludwig-gleim.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Johann Wilhelm Ludwig Gleim</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Johann Wilhelm Ludwig Gleim was a German poet of the Anacreontic school who served from 1747 as secretary of the cathedral chapter at Halberstadt.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/johann-wilhelm-duke-of-saxe-eisenach"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/johann-wilhelm-duke-of-saxe-eisenach.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Johann Wilhelm, Duke of Saxe-Eisenach</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Johann Wilhelm, Duke of Saxe-Eisenach, ruled the small Ernestine duchy from 1698 to 1729.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/johann-wolfgang-von-goethe"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/johann-wolfgang-von-goethe.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Johann Wolfgang von Goethe</span><span clas