"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/johannes-ciconia.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Johannes Ciconia</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Johannes Ciconia was a composer born in Liège who worked in Italy, above all in Padua, where he died in 1412.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/johannes-gutenberg"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/johannes-gutenberg.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Johannes Gutenberg</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Johannes Gutenberg was a fifteenth-century German craftsman who developed printing with movable metal type in Europe.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/johannes-hoffmann"><span class="enc-name">Johannes Hoffmann</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Johannes Hoffmann was a German Social Democratic politician who became Minister-President of Bavaria in March 1919, after the murder of Kurt Eisner.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/johannes-kabin"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/johannes-kabin.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Johannes Käbin</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Johannes Käbin was an Estonian communist, raised in Russia, who served as First Secretary of the Communist Party of Estonia from 1950 to 1978.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/johannes-kepler"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/johannes-kepler.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Johannes Kepler</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Johannes Kepler was a German astronomer and mathematician, born in the imperial city of Weil der Stadt, who discovered the three laws of planetary motion.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/johannes-sturm"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/johannes-sturm.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Johannes Sturm</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Johannes Sturm was a German humanist and educator who founded the Protestant Gymnasium of Strasbourg in 1538 and directed it for more than 40 years.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/johannes-trithemius"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/johannes-trithemius.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Johannes Trithemius</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Johannes Trithemius was a German Benedictine abbot, humanist and scholar, abbot of Sponheim from 1483 and later of St James at Würzburg.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/johannes-van-den-bosch"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/johannes-van-den-bosch.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Johannes van den Bosch</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Johannes van den Bosch was a Dutch Governor-General of the Dutch East Indies.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/johannes-van-walbeeck"><span class="enc-name">Johannes van Walbeeck</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Johannes van Walbeeck was a Dutch navigator and commander of the Dutch West India Company.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/johannes-vermeer"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/johannes-vermeer.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Johannes Vermeer</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Johannes Vermeer was a Dutch painter who worked in Delft.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/johannesburg"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/johannesburg.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Johannesburg</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Johannesburg is the largest city of South Africa and the capital of Gauteng province.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/johannisburg-palace"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/johannisburg-palace.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Johannisburg Palace</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">Johannisburg Palace is a Renaissance palace of red sandstone on the Main at Aschaffenburg in Bavaria, built between 1605 and 1614 by the architect Georg Ridinger for…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/john-a-macdonald"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/john-a-macdonald.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">John A. Macdonald</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">John A. Macdonald was a Scottish-born Canadian lawyer and Conservative politician, a leader of the Great Coalition of 1864 in the Province of Canada and the main…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/john-albert-ii-duke-of-mecklenburg"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/john-albert-ii-duke-of-mecklenburg.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">John Albert II, Duke of Mecklenburg</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">John Albert II, Duke of Mecklenburg, shared rule of Mecklenburg with his brother Adolf Frederick I, and their partition of 1621 created the Duchy of Mecklenburg-Güstrow.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/john-aleman"><span class="enc-name">John Aleman</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">John Aleman was the last lord of Caesarea in the crusader Kingdom of Jerusalem, holding the lordship through his marriage to its heiress Margaret.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/john-amos-comenius"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/john-amos-comenius.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">John Amos Comenius</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">John Amos Comenius was a Czech philosopher, educator and the last bishop of the Unity of the Brethren.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/john-ball"><span class="enc-name">John Ball</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">John Ball was an English priest whose preaching on the equality of all people made him one of the leaders of the Peasants' Revolt of 1381.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/john-balliol"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/john-balliol.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">John Balliol</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">John Balliol was king of Scotland from 1292 to 1296, chosen by Edward I of England from among the claimants after the death of Margaret, Maid of Norway.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/john-barbour"><span class="enc-name">John Barbour</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">John Barbour was a Scottish poet and churchman, archdeacon of Aberdeen from 1357.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/john-bates-thurston"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/john-bates-thurston.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">John Bates Thurston</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sir John Bates Thurston was a British colonial official who served as a minister in Cakobau's Kingdom of Fiji before the cession of 1874.</span></a></li></ul>
